  "title": "Second-order log-Laplace expansion at zero for hyperbolic-space random walks and horofunction cocycles with the CLT variance coefficient",
  "difficulty_level": "H2",
  "difficulty_reason": "One integrated H2 remains after the named Freedman bounded-increment inequality and two-point reduction, Benoist-Quint/Horbez/Aoun-Sert bounded-cohomology martingale decomposition, BMSS punctual deviations, and Burkholder/Azuma tools are allowed. A CLT alone does not control the exponential growth rate when n tends to infinity first: rare unbounded jumps can alter the Laplace curvature. The authors match an explicit large-jump tail compensation to truncated predictable variation and obtain the uniform second-moment comparison required for a merely measurable variance function on the horofunction compactification. The bracket-tail estimate then makes the compensation vanish in the required nested limits, while convolution acceleration replaces the worst-starting-point variance bound by the actual CLT variance. These dependent lower/upper mechanisms close one sharp second-order coefficient. Conditional Taylor expansion, Holder, block Azuma, scaling and convex duality are supporting steps and do not justify separate counts or H3.",
  "source_id": "doi:10.5802/jep.225",
